/-!
# The higher-codimension Gysin formula in the expected dimension

Let `(S, δ)` be the base situation of chapter 42 of the Stacks Project, let `X` be a scheme
locally of finite type over `S`, let `Z ⊆ X` be a closed subscheme carrying a virtual normal
sheaf `𝒩`, and let `Y ⟶ X` be locally of finite type. **Stacks 0FEA** asserts that if

1. `𝒩` is locally free of rank `r`,
2. every irreducible component of `Y` has `δ`-dimension `n`,
3. `dim_δ(Z ×_X Y) ≤ n - r`, and
4. `𝒪_{Y, ξ}` is Cohen–Macaulay for every `ξ ∈ Z ×_X Y` with `δ(ξ) = n - r`,

then `c(Z ⟶ X, 𝒩) ∩ [Y]_n = [Z ×_X Y]_{n - r}`.

What this module proves is the step of the source's proof that carries its content, namely that
hypotheses (1)–(4) make `Z ×_X Y` a *regular* closed subscheme of `Y` at the relevant points:
at every `ξ ∈ Z ×_X Y` with `δ(ξ) = n - r`, the ideal of `Z ×_X Y` in the local ring `𝒪_{Y, ξ}`
is generated by a regular sequence of length `r`. The source deduces this from Algebra, Lemma
10.104.2 (Stacks 02JN) after computing `dim(𝒪_{Y, ξ}) = r` from (2) and (3), and uses it to
reduce the lemma to the excess intersection formula of Stacks 0FBM over a neighbourhood of `ξ`
on which the excess normal sheaf vanishes.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Chow.coheight_eq_toNat_sub_relativeDimension` and
  `Stacks.Chow.ringKrullDim_stalk_eq_toNat_sub_relativeDimension` — on a scheme all of whose
  irreducible components have `δ`-dimension `n`, the local ring at a point of `δ`-dimension `m`
  has Krull dimension `n - m`. This is the source's step "by assumption (2) we see that
  `dim(𝒪_{Y, ξ}) = r`".
* `Stacks.Chow.isRegular_of_isCohenMacaulayLocalRing_stalk` — **Stacks 0FEA**, the regular
  sequence: under (1)–(4) a list of `r` elements of `𝒪_{Y, ξ}` generating the ideal of
  `Z ×_X Y` is a regular sequence.

## Implementation notes

**What is not stated.** The cap-product identity `c(Z ⟶ X, 𝒩) ∩ [Y]_n = [Z ×_X Y]_{n - r}` is
not stated here, and could not honestly be. The bivariant class `c(Z ⟶ X, 𝒩)` of Stacks 0FBK
has no carrier in this library — the implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.DiagonalGysin` record its absence, which is also why the
formalization of Stacks 0FFD stops at the verification of the hypotheses of this lemma. Taking
the class as a parameter is not a way round this: for an *arbitrary*
`Stacks.Chow.BivariantClass` of degree `r` the identity is
false, so a statement with the class universally quantified would have to carry a hypothesis
pinning it down, and the only such hypothesis available — the excess intersection formula of
Stacks 0FBM at vanishing excess — *is* the conclusion, read over an open subscheme.

The two remaining steps of the source's proof are also unavailable. Globalizing from a
neighbourhood of `ξ` to `Z ×_X Y` uses the description of `CH_{n-r}(Z ×_X Y)` as the free group
on the components of `δ`-dimension `n - r`, valid because of (3); this library has no such
description (tags 02RX and 02RE here are the localization sequence and flat pullback). And
spreading the regular sequence from the local ring to a neighbourhood, Divisors Lemma 31.21.4,
is available — it is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.exists_mem_isQuasiRegularImmersion_of_isRegular_germ` (Stacks 063I) —
but what it would feed is the missing class.

**Hypothesis (1).** Local freeness of rank `r` enters the source only through its consequence
that the ideal sheaf of `Z` is locally cut out by `r` equations, hence that the ideal of
`Z ×_X Y` in `𝒪_Y` is locally generated by `r` elements. That consequence is what is assumed
here, in the form of a list `xs` of `r` elements of `𝒪_{Y, ξ}` generating the ideal; deriving it
from a `Stacks.Chow.VirtualNormalBundle` of rank `r` needs the surjection
`𝒩^∨ ⟶ 𝒞_{Z/X}` of Stacks 0FBQ, its base change along `Y ⟶ X` (Stacks 0FBJ,
`Stacks.Chow.VirtualNormalBundle.baseChange`) and Nakayama, none of which is about the regular
sequence this module is after. The hypothesis says only that the ideal is *generated* by `r`
elements; that those elements form a *regular* sequence is the conclusion.

**The dimension computation.** `dim(𝒪_{Y, ξ}) = r` is proved topologically. The Krull dimension
of a stalk is the coheight of the point (Stacks 02IZ), the relative dimension function of
Stacks 02JW is a dimension function, and the codimension formula of Stacks 02IA bounds chains
through a point by the drop of that function. Hypothesis (2) caps the function at `n`, which
bounds the coheight above, and the extremal chain of Stacks 02IA realizes the bound. The
repository proves the codimension-one case of this,
`Stacks.Chow.coheight_eq_one_of_relativeDimension_eq_sub_one`, and the general case in a
`private` form inside `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Chow.RelativeDimension` for an integral
scheme; neither is usable here, so it is proved again, for a scheme that need not be integral.

**Ideals of definition.** Hypothesis (3) together with `δ(ξ) = n - r` makes `ξ` the generic
point of an irreducible component of `Z ×_X Y`, by Stacks 02QT, so the stalk of `Z ×_X Y` at
`ξ` has Krull dimension zero — which is the source's "`𝓘_ξ` is an ideal of definition". That
stalk is the quotient of `𝒪_{Y, ξ}` by the ideal, the stalk map of a closed immersion being
surjective.

